    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (80.98) stands as our clear, high-ceiling anchor. Facing Pallante, his elite speed is weaponized by the opposing pitcher\u2019s slow delivery, maximizing our scarcity edge in stolen bases. Alongside him, **Junior Caminero** (71.40) and **Yordan Alvarez** (70.14) provide the necessary thump in the heart of the order. **Caminero** benefits from a strong park factor and a favorable matchup against Ure\u00f1a, while **Alvarez** remains an auto-start despite a modest park handicap, given his elite xwOBA of 0.425. We also expect significant value from **Kazuma Okamoto** (67.57), whose strong platoon edge against Rogers and favorable park adjustments elevate him well above his base projection.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench was excluding **Caleb Durbin** (50.93). While he offers premium speed and a boost against slow pitchers, his projected spot at the bottom of the order (-8% penalty) fundamentally capped his efficiency compared to our starters. Consequently, he remains our primary pivot if any last-minute lineup shifts occur. Meanwhile, we continue to monitor the status of **Hyeseong Kim**, who remains sidelined in the minors, and **Colt Keith**, who is not in today\u2019s starting rotation. \n\nRisk remains concentrated in the pitching matchups for **Carson Benge** and **Kyle Schwarber**. Both face the lefty Wrobleski; while **Schwarber** maintains a respectable 58.23 efficiency thanks to his high xwOBA and leadoff positioning, the matchup against a 3.49 xERA pitcher limits the ceiling for both hitters. We are avoiding any exposure to **Gary Sanchez**, **Zac Veen**, **Lars Nootbaar**, **Josh Smith**, **Luis Pena**, or **Pedro Ramirez** today, as their lack of confirmed starts or unfavorable platoon splits relative to the active roster keeps them firmly on the bench.",
